Z||000003||Oreif||01-09-2001||01:41 AM||||First, How big is the amp? You also want to make sure that the vents you put in won't get wet. If they are too big, or open too much, the front tires could splash water up into them from underneath. Putting the vents by the radiator wall would probably be bad since the heat from the radiator would be there. Let us know where you end up putting them.||||reg||1
Z||000004||varnster||01-09-2001||10:12 PM||||Its a 75x4 pushing infinity kapa 4x6 fronts<BR>and 4x6 infinity referance in the rear. It<BR>sits just above the brake master cyl, with<BR>about a 16th inch clearance between the hood<BR>and the amp with the fins fully exposed under<BR>the enclosure, what about a little 12volt recirc fan behind the driver side head lamp,<BR>but I HATE TO CUT MY CAR its like cutting my<BR>own throat.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||01-10-2001||07:30 AM||||When I mounted my 100x4 amp and 10 CD changer on a piece of 3/4" plywood over the spare tire area, I did the following to move air around the amp:<P>The bottom of the amp was so close to the plywood that little air could flow under it. I routed out an area about 3/8" deep under the amp.  I also cut a hole in the center of that area and mounted an old PC power supply fan under it (on the backside of the plywood base) to blow air into that area.<P>It was like that for several months in 90+ degree NW Florida heat and never got more than warm.  I have since removed the amp because I could never filter out all the whining noises from the alternator, ecm, etc.  The CD changer is still mounted there.  To remove the whole thing to get to the spare tire I have one connector to remove from the changer and 3 wingnuts that mount the plywood shelf.  It has worked out really well.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||01-10-2001||07:58 PM||||Varnster...<P>I have my amp mounted in an enclosed box above my spare tire.  The amp has cooling fans built in.  I have 3/4" holes at the back of the box to allow air in, and a couple holes near the amp's cooling fan outlet.  I've never had problems with heat, and my amp has have never shut down because of thermal overload, even during competition.  If you amp has no cooling fan, you may want to use a brushless 12v fan in the amp enclosure to exhaust heat buildup.<P>Please post a photo of your setup if possible.<P>My install at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierosound</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 01-10-2001).]||||reg||10
